# Line endings are part of HRW's test contract, not a formatting preference.
#
# Several tests read repository files as EXACT TEXT — the tour catalogue is
# compared byte-for-byte against freshly generated Markdown, and `app.rs` is
# parsed by splitting on "\n}\n". Both assume LF.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its SYSTEM config
# (`C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig`), so a fresh clone on a stock Windows
# box silently rewrites every checked-out file to CRLF. Two tests then fail,
# and one of them fails for a reason that is FALSE:
#
#   app::tests::tour_catalogue_is_current
#       diffs CATALOGUE.md on disk against text generated with "\n".
#       Fails honestly — the message names the catalogue.
#
#   doc_citations::tests::app_does_not_regrow_its_field_count
#       splits app.rs on "\n}\n" and reports
#       "the App struct must be closed by a `}` at column 0".
#       The struct IS closed correctly; the line is "}\r". A session reading
#       that message goes looking for an app.rs defect that does not exist.
#
# Found 2026-08-07, on the second Windows machine. `git config core.autocrlf
# false` fixes one clone; this file fixes every clone — the difference the
# repository exists to make (`DECISIONS.md`, "the repository is the system of
# record", and the same lesson as the memory store that did not survive a move).
#
# SCOPED TO hrw/ DELIBERATELY. Measured the same day: all 41 files stored with
# CRLF in the index live under `crates/rumoca-*`. Normalizing upstream files
# would put line-ending churn in a PR to CogniPilot, so this file governs HRW
# only and an upstream cherry-pick stays clean.
#
# `text=auto` lets git classify text vs binary itself; `eol=lf` pins the
# working-tree ending for whatever it classifies as text.

* text=auto eol=lf
